\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Odd translation and exact source completion}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Replacing any field generator by itself plus a source-only
odd element preserves the \vocabk{ring paired coefficient}{fdip-00TJ}
in the source algebra. If #{\det K\in k^\times}, the
\vocabk{ring source polynomial}{fdip-00TN} satisfies
##{\mathscr Z_K(\bar\eta,\eta)
 =\det K\,\extexp\bigl(\bar\eta(K^{-1}\eta)\bigr).}}

\proof{
\p{Let #{\tau} fix every source generator and send a field
generator #{z_a} to #{z_a+s_a}, with #{s_a} source-only and odd.
The odd-square and anticommutation identities proved in
\ref{fdip-00TH} give an exterior-algebra homomorphism.
Subtracting the same #{s_a} gives its inverse. Any word using
at least one replacement has fewer field generators, so cannot
contribute to the field top coefficient. Hence
#{\mathcal I_V(\tau P)=\mathcal I_V(P)} for every #{P}.
Naturality from \ref{fdip-00TH} separately gives
#{\extexp(\tau a)=\tau\extexp(a)}.}

\p{For a finite free module, #{\det K} is a unit exactly
when #{K} is invertible: the adjugate identity gives the inverse
in one direction, and multiplicativity of the determinant gives
the other. Put
#{\chi=\psi-K^{-1}\eta} and
#{\bar\chi=\bar\psi-\bar\eta K^{-1}}. Expansion in the
written order gives
##{\begin{aligned}
 -\bar\psi K\psi+\bar\eta\psi+\bar\psi\eta
 &=-\bar\chi K\chi+\bar\eta K^{-1}\eta.
\end{aligned}}
The two terms on the right are even. Multiplicativity of the
finite polynomial, invariance under the odd translation and
\ref{fdip-00TL} give the stated formula, with the source-only
factor pulled out of #{\mathcal I_V}.}
}
